1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:06,730
In April 1989, a woman out running in
Central Park at night was attacked,

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:08,240
beaten and left close to death.

3
00:00:08,260 --> 00:00:12,240
Within about two days, police and
prosecutors had statements from

4
00:00:12,260 --> 00:00:14,730
five teenagers. The
youngest were fourteen.

5
00:00:17,200 --> 00:00:19,610
Four of them described the
attack on videotape.

6
00:00:20,140 --> 00:00:23,790
Each put himself at the scene, and put
the worst of it on the others.

7
00:00:24,440 --> 00:00:29,430
The fifth, Yusef Salaam, who was
fifteen, never went on tape.

8
00:00:31,510 --> 00:00:34,910
Before he talked, a detective told him
his fingerprints could turn

9
00:00:34,930 --> 00:00:35,900
up on her clothing.

10
00:00:36,410 --> 00:00:37,520
There were no such prints.

11
00:00:38,150 --> 00:00:41,260
At trial, the detective
acknowledged it had been a trick.

12
00:00:43,650 --> 00:00:46,420
No physical evidence tied any
of the five to her.

13
00:00:47,090 --> 00:00:50,080
The DNA found at the scene
matched none of them.

14
00:00:50,610 --> 00:00:55,250
They were convicted anyway, in 1990, and
spent from about six to about

15
00:00:55,270 --> 00:00:56,780
thirteen years locked up.

16
00:00:58,740 --> 00:01:03,140
In 2002, a man serving life for other
crimes said he had attacked

17
00:01:03,160 --> 00:01:06,170
her, alone. His DNA matched.

18
00:01:06,940 --> 00:01:09,920
The Manhattan district attorney asked
the court to throw out every

19
00:01:09,940 --> 00:01:11,630
conviction, and it did.

20
00:01:12,300 --> 00:01:15,270
Today the five men are known as
the Exonerated Five.

21
00:01:17,390 --> 00:01:20,970
And what that detective did is
something police in every American

22
00:01:20,990 --> 00:01:23,060
state may still do to adults.

23
00:01:24,030 --> 00:01:25,800
Lying about the evidence is allowed.

24
00:01:26,150 --> 00:01:30,180
Since 2021, ten states have
restricted it for children.

25
00:01:32,680 --> 00:01:35,570
So, should police be allowed to lie
to the people they question?

26
00:01:36,140 --> 00:01:39,750
A bluff about the evidence can get a
guilty person to tell the truth.

27
00:01:40,400 --> 00:01:43,640
It can also convince an innocent one
that confessing is the only way

28
00:01:43,660 --> 00:01:47,490
out. And does the answer change when the
person in the room is fifteen?

29
00:01:50,470 --> 00:01:54,670
We went through the district
attorney's own 2002 filing, the police

30
00:01:54,690 --> 00:01:58,300
department's report that disagreed with
it, the Supreme Court's rulings,

31
00:01:58,650 --> 00:02:01,700
the new state laws, and the
research on false confessions.

32
00:02:02,390 --> 00:02:06,360
At the end, we'll give you a verdict,
and tell you how sure we are.

33
00:02:18,506 --> 00:02:22,906
On the night of April the nineteenth,
1989, a group of thirty or more

34
00:02:22,926 --> 00:02:25,696
teenagers came into the north
end of Central Park.

35
00:02:26,266 --> 00:02:29,676
Over the next forty minutes or so,
some of them attacked strangers.

36
00:02:30,066 --> 00:02:32,996
A man was beaten and robbed
and left unconscious.

37
00:02:33,906 --> 00:02:35,816
Joggers at the reservoir
were set upon.

38
00:02:36,406 --> 00:02:39,786
Those attacks were real, and some of
the five would later admit to

39
00:02:39,806 --> 00:02:40,596
parts of them.

40
00:02:42,236 --> 00:02:43,196
Trisha Meili was twenty-nine.

41
00:02:43,216 --> 00:02:46,216
She went out for a run at
about nine o'clock.

42
00:02:46,236 --> 00:02:49,936
She was found after half past one in
the morning, near a footpath

43
00:02:49,956 --> 00:02:53,336
in the north of the park,
beaten and close to death.

44
00:02:53,356 --> 00:02:57,196
She survived. She has no
memory of the attack.

45
00:02:57,216 --> 00:03:00,586
For fourteen years she kept
her name private.

46
00:03:01,456 --> 00:03:05,106
Since 2003, she has spoken
under her own name.

47
00:03:07,866 --> 00:03:10,866
Police picked up teenagers from the
group that night and the next

48
00:03:10,886 --> 00:03:15,926
day. Under New York law at the time,
anyone under sixteen could only

49
00:03:15,946 --> 00:03:18,516
be questioned with a parent
or guardian present.

50
00:03:19,566 --> 00:03:22,076
At sixteen, you were
questioned as an adult.

51
00:03:23,896 --> 00:03:26,756
Five of them were questioned about
the attack on Trisha Meili.

52
00:03:26,776 --> 00:03:30,136
Kevin Richardson and Raymond
Santana were fourteen.

53
00:03:30,156 --> 00:03:35,166
Antron McCray and Yusef
Salaam were fifteen.

54
00:03:36,256 --> 00:03:39,876
Korey Wise was sixteen, so he was
questioned with no parent in the

55
00:03:39,896 --> 00:03:40,286
room.

56
00:03:43,386 --> 00:03:46,266
Prosecutors came to the precinct, and
the district attorney's office

57
00:03:46,286 --> 00:03:48,616
recorded the final
statements on video.

58
00:03:49,386 --> 00:03:53,046
The Manhattan district attorney,
Robert Morgenthau, was a pioneer

59
00:03:53,066 --> 00:03:54,836
of taping confessions.

60
00:03:55,666 --> 00:03:57,556
But the camera caught only the end.

61
00:03:57,966 --> 00:04:01,306
The hours of questioning before it
weren't recorded, and what happened

62
00:04:01,326 --> 00:04:02,836
in them is still disputed.

63
00:04:05,756 --> 00:04:08,376
Yusef Salaam first said he
hadn't been there.

64
00:04:08,396 --> 00:04:11,816
Then a detective told him his
fingerprints could be on her clothing.

65
00:04:11,836 --> 00:04:16,836
Accounts differ on the exact words,
but there were no such prints.

66
00:04:16,856 --> 00:04:19,966
Salaam then gave an account, which
the detective wrote down.

67
00:04:20,436 --> 00:04:23,806
He never signed it, and he has
always said he never confessed.

68
00:04:26,366 --> 00:04:28,696
On tape, none of the four
admitted the rape.

69
00:04:29,146 --> 00:04:32,516
Each gave an account in which, in the
district attorney's later words,

70
00:04:32,686 --> 00:04:35,496
he made himself an accomplice,
and named the others.

71
00:04:37,896 --> 00:04:41,456
Before trial, the boys and their
parents testified that detectives

72
00:04:41,476 --> 00:04:45,316
yelled at them, called them liars, and
that some were told they could

73
00:04:45,336 --> 00:04:46,726
go home if they talked.

74
00:04:47,216 --> 00:04:48,866
The judge didn't believe them.

75
00:04:49,136 --> 00:04:53,616
He found no improper trickery, and
allowed every statement in, except

76
00:04:53,636 --> 00:04:54,626
one remark.

77
00:04:56,426 --> 00:04:58,796
So, the first weight on the scale.

78
00:04:59,946 --> 00:05:03,266
Five boys, the youngest fourteen.

79
00:05:03,286 --> 00:05:06,506
Hours of questioning that
no one recorded.

80
00:05:06,526 --> 00:05:09,636
One lie that we know of, told
to a fifteen-year-old.

81
00:05:10,226 --> 00:05:14,316
And at the end of it, four videotapes
that two juries would hear.

82
00:05:20,418 --> 00:05:22,268
There were two trials in 1990.

83
00:05:22,838 --> 00:05:27,878
In August, Antron McCray, Yusef Salaam
and Raymond Santana were convicted

84
00:05:27,898 --> 00:05:30,768
of rape, assault, robbery and riot.

85
00:05:31,758 --> 00:05:35,168
In December, Kevin Richardson was
convicted on every count.

86
00:05:36,118 --> 00:05:40,238
Korey Wise was convicted of assault,
sexual abuse and riot, but not

87
00:05:40,258 --> 00:05:40,908
of rape.

88
00:05:42,788 --> 00:05:45,848
Thirteen years later, the district
attorney's own office looked back

89
00:05:45,868 --> 00:05:50,168
and said it plainly: at both trials,
the case rested almost entirely

90
00:05:50,188 --> 00:05:51,038
on the statements.

91
00:05:53,178 --> 00:05:56,138
There was no physical or forensic
evidence connecting them to the

92
00:05:56,158 --> 00:05:57,178
attack on Trisha Meili.

93
00:05:57,198 --> 00:06:01,998
Semen found at the scene came from one
man, and before trial it was

94
00:06:02,018 --> 00:06:06,008
known to match none of the five, and
none of the boys they had named.

95
00:06:06,938 --> 00:06:10,328
Prosecutors argued there must have been
another attacker who got away.

96
00:06:11,388 --> 00:06:13,198
And the statements didn't agree.

97
00:06:13,888 --> 00:06:18,048
When the district attorney's office
reviewed them in 2002, it found

98
00:06:18,068 --> 00:06:22,388
they differed on virtually every major
part of the crime: who started

99
00:06:22,408 --> 00:06:28,668
it, who knocked her down, who held
her, what weapons were used, and

100
00:06:28,688 --> 00:06:29,498
when it happened.

101
00:06:31,158 --> 00:06:35,678
Most significant, it said, none of
them correctly described where

102
00:06:35,698 --> 00:06:36,808
the attack happened.

103
00:06:37,778 --> 00:06:41,318
Korey Wise came nearest, but police had
taken him to the scene before

104
00:06:41,338 --> 00:06:44,908
he went on tape. Some details
were simply wrong.

105
00:06:45,518 --> 00:06:48,208
One statement described knife
wounds she didn't have.

106
00:06:50,208 --> 00:06:52,798
Why would anyone admit to
something they didn't do?

107
00:06:53,568 --> 00:06:56,588
In a well-known experiment, students
were falsely accused of crashing

108
00:06:56,608 --> 00:07:00,348
a computer. When someone claimed to
have seen them do it, the share

109
00:07:00,368 --> 00:07:03,838
who signed a confession rose from
forty-eight percent to ninety-four.

110
00:07:05,898 --> 00:07:09,428
And people watching can't reliably
spot a false confession.

111
00:07:10,018 --> 00:07:14,258
In one study, observers who judged
true and false confessions did

112
00:07:14,278 --> 00:07:15,488
no better than chance.

113
00:07:16,908 --> 00:07:18,608
Young people are the most exposed.

114
00:07:18,628 --> 00:07:23,268
In a study of a hundred and
twenty-five proven false confessions,

115
00:07:23,288 --> 00:07:25,968
about a third came from juveniles.

116
00:07:25,988 --> 00:07:30,488
In another, of people wrongly convicted
for crimes committed as juveniles,

117
00:07:30,508 --> 00:07:33,208
forty-two percent had
falsely confessed.

118
00:07:33,228 --> 00:07:36,328
That study also counted the false
confessions behind wrongful rape

119
00:07:36,348 --> 00:07:39,698
convictions. More than half
came from this one case.

120
00:07:42,318 --> 00:07:46,918
Across the three hundred and seventy-five
DNA exonerations the Innocence

121
00:07:46,938 --> 00:07:50,348
Project has counted, about three in ten
involved a false confession.

122
00:07:51,708 --> 00:07:53,118
So, the second weight.

123
00:07:53,728 --> 00:07:54,738
No physical evidence.

124
00:07:55,488 --> 00:07:58,038
Statements that contradicted
each other and the scene.

125
00:07:58,848 --> 00:08:02,428
And research showing that the pull toward
a false confession is strongest

126
00:08:02,448 --> 00:08:07,258
on the young. Still, two juries
heard all of it, and convicted.

127
00:08:13,435 --> 00:08:17,835
In late 2001, a man serving
thirty-three years to life, for rapes

128
00:08:17,855 --> 00:08:22,475
and a murder he committed later that
summer of 1989, told prison staff

129
00:08:22,495 --> 00:08:25,645
that someone else had been convicted
of a crime he'd committed.

130
00:08:26,175 --> 00:08:27,965
His name was Matias Reyes.

131
00:08:28,695 --> 00:08:32,305
He said he had attacked Trisha Meili,
and that he had done it alone.

132
00:08:32,795 --> 00:08:36,135
He said a chance meeting with Korey
Wise, in prison, had prompted

133
00:08:36,155 --> 00:08:38,385
him. Wise was still inside.

134
00:08:39,705 --> 00:08:44,725
In May 2002, the FBI reported that his
DNA matched the semen on her

135
00:08:44,745 --> 00:08:48,825
sock. Further tests matched him to the
other samples, with odds of

136
00:08:48,845 --> 00:08:50,935
one in six billion, and to a hair.

137
00:08:51,585 --> 00:08:53,395
No one else's DNA was present.

138
00:08:55,475 --> 00:08:58,885
His account fit the scene in ways none
of the five statements had:

139
00:08:59,275 --> 00:09:03,255
the direction she was running, where
she was struck, the things that

140
00:09:03,275 --> 00:09:04,505
were taken from her.

141
00:09:04,915 --> 00:09:08,505
And two days earlier, he had attacked
another woman in the park.

142
00:09:10,005 --> 00:09:14,345
In December 2002, the district
attorney's office asked the court to

143
00:09:14,365 --> 00:09:19,005
throw out every conviction: not only the
rape, but the robbery, assault

144
00:09:19,025 --> 00:09:23,655
and riot convictions too, because they
all rested on the same statements,

145
00:09:24,105 --> 00:09:25,735
taken in the same questioning.

146
00:09:26,245 --> 00:09:30,675
The office still called the other crimes
that night grave and inexcusable.

147
00:09:31,445 --> 00:09:35,595
On December the nineteenth, Justice
Charles Tejada vacated them all.

148
00:09:36,205 --> 00:09:38,115
Korey Wise had been
released that August.

149
00:09:39,615 --> 00:09:41,825
Not everyone accepted it.

150
00:09:42,335 --> 00:09:46,275
In January 2003, a panel appointed by
the police commissioner found

151
00:09:46,295 --> 00:09:47,665
no police misconduct.

152
00:09:48,215 --> 00:09:51,395
It concluded it was more likely than
not that the five took part in

153
00:09:51,415 --> 00:09:54,585
the attack, and that Reyes joined
in or came afterwards.

154
00:09:55,255 --> 00:09:58,305
It pointed out that nothing but his
word said he acted alone.

155
00:09:58,735 --> 00:10:00,285
No court has heard that theory.

156
00:10:00,995 --> 00:10:04,595
Linda Fairstein, who ran the district
attorney's sex crimes unit in

157
00:10:04,615 --> 00:10:07,785
1989, has also disputed
the exoneration.

158
00:10:10,265 --> 00:10:13,405
Trisha Meili has always said she
remembers nothing of the attack.

159
00:10:13,425 --> 00:10:17,385
In 2019, she said she wished the case
hadn't been settled, and that

160
00:10:17,405 --> 00:10:20,605
she had always believed more than one
person was involved, because

161
00:10:20,625 --> 00:10:22,775
of DNA that was never identified.

162
00:10:25,395 --> 00:10:29,595
In 2014, New York City settled the men's
lawsuit for forty-one million

163
00:10:29,615 --> 00:10:31,765
dollars, denying any wrongdoing.

164
00:10:32,315 --> 00:10:36,975
In 2022, an entrance to Central Park was
named the Gate of the Exonerated.

165
00:10:36,995 --> 00:10:41,065
And Yusef Salaam now represents Harlem
on the New York City Council.

166
00:10:42,265 --> 00:10:43,535
So, the third weight.

167
00:10:44,265 --> 00:10:47,885
The DNA identified a man none of the
statements mentioned, whose account

168
00:10:47,905 --> 00:10:51,425
fit the scene. Whatever you believe
about the rest of that night,

169
00:10:51,445 --> 00:10:54,605
the confessions were not a reliable
account of the attack on Trisha

170
00:10:54,625 --> 00:10:57,715
Meili. And for thirteen
years, they were enough.

171
00:11:04,070 --> 00:11:08,800
The American rule goes back to 1969,
and a case called Frazier versus

172
00:11:09,030 --> 00:11:13,670
Cupp. Police had told a suspect,
falsely, that his cousin had already

173
00:11:13,690 --> 00:11:18,410
confessed. The Supreme Court said the
lie was relevant, but not enough

174
00:11:18,430 --> 00:11:20,480
on its own to throw out
his confession.

175
00:11:21,410 --> 00:11:24,200
Judges should look at the
totality of the circumstances.

176
00:11:25,320 --> 00:11:29,060
But the Court noted that the
questioning had been short, and that

177
00:11:29,080 --> 00:11:34,690
the suspect was, in its words, a mature
individual of normal intelligence.

178
00:11:35,520 --> 00:11:39,100
The rule was written about an adult,
and for decades it was applied

179
00:11:39,120 --> 00:11:45,060
to children too. In 2011, the Court said
age does matter: a reasonable

180
00:11:45,080 --> 00:11:49,340
child, it wrote, will sometimes feel
pressured to submit when a reasonable

181
00:11:49,360 --> 00:11:51,210
adult would feel free to go.

182
00:11:52,790 --> 00:11:54,760
England had already gone another way.

183
00:11:55,590 --> 00:11:59,930
In 1987, two years before Central
Park, the Court of Appeal heard

184
00:11:59,950 --> 00:12:03,770
a case in which police told a suspect,
and his solicitor, that his

185
00:12:03,790 --> 00:12:06,700
fingerprint had been found in
a very telling place.

186
00:12:07,150 --> 00:12:08,400
They had invented it.

187
00:12:08,810 --> 00:12:11,540
The court ruled his confession
should have been excluded.

188
00:12:12,210 --> 00:12:16,450
Under a 1984 law, English courts can
refuse evidence that would make

189
00:12:16,470 --> 00:12:20,810
a trial unfair, and must exclude a
confession obtained in a way likely

190
00:12:20,830 --> 00:12:24,700
to make it unreliable, even if
it turns out to be true.

191
00:12:26,880 --> 00:12:31,460
England's own law came out of a false
confession: three boys, aged

192
00:12:31,480 --> 00:12:36,770
fourteen to eighteen, confessed to a
murder in 1972 they hadn't committed.

193
00:12:37,180 --> 00:12:40,880
After more wrongful convictions,
English police built a new way of

194
00:12:40,900 --> 00:12:45,080
interviewing, called PEACE, around
getting an account rather than

195
00:12:45,100 --> 00:12:50,360
a confession. According to a 2025 review
by leading researchers, lying

196
00:12:50,380 --> 00:12:55,170
about the evidence is now barred in
England, Germany, Japan, Australia,

197
00:12:55,420 --> 00:12:57,090
the Nordic countries and others.

198
00:12:58,830 --> 00:13:01,620
In the United States, the change
has come for children.

199
00:13:02,070 --> 00:13:06,930
In July 2021, Illinois and Oregon
became the first states to restrict

200
00:13:06,950 --> 00:13:09,520
police lying to minors in
interrogations.

201
00:13:10,030 --> 00:13:11,320
Eight more have followed.

202
00:13:11,810 --> 00:13:13,660
Most don't ban the lie outright.

203
00:13:14,410 --> 00:13:18,620
If police lie about the evidence, or
promise leniency they can't give,

204
00:13:18,790 --> 00:13:22,710
the confession that follows is presumed
involuntary, and prosecutors

205
00:13:22,730 --> 00:13:24,420
have to prove it wasn't.

206
00:13:26,020 --> 00:13:27,930
For adults, nothing has changed.

207
00:13:28,580 --> 00:13:32,240
In every state, police may still tell
you they have evidence they

208
00:13:32,260 --> 00:13:37,540
don't. Bills to change that, in New
York and Washington State, have

209
00:13:37,560 --> 00:13:38,090
stalled.

210
00:13:40,350 --> 00:13:41,840
So, the fourth weight.

211
00:13:42,550 --> 00:13:46,870
American law still allows the lie,
and trusts judges to catch the

212
00:13:46,890 --> 00:13:47,1000
confessions it breaks.

213
00:13:48,490 --> 00:13:53,260
England decided that wasn't enough, and
changed how police ask questions.

214
00:13:54,210 --> 00:13:56,700
And ten states have now
drawn a line at age.

215
00:14:02,796 --> 00:14:06,126
First, the strongest case for
letting police lie.

216
00:14:06,936 --> 00:14:11,226
Most people police question are guilty,
and most don't want to talk.

217
00:14:11,916 --> 00:14:15,596
A bluff about the evidence is often
what moves a guilty person from

218
00:14:15,616 --> 00:14:17,106
silence to the truth.

219
00:14:18,076 --> 00:14:21,356
Police chiefs say it produces many
more true confessions than false

220
00:14:21,376 --> 00:14:26,026
ones. In one lab study, pressure tactics
nearly doubled true confessions.

221
00:14:26,436 --> 00:14:30,496
The law already has a safety valve: a
judge must find every confession

222
00:14:30,516 --> 00:14:32,986
voluntary, and appeals courts check.

223
00:14:33,756 --> 00:14:37,716
In this very case, the trial judge heard
weeks of testimony and believed

224
00:14:37,736 --> 00:14:41,516
the detectives, and the police
department's own panel still believes

225
00:14:41,536 --> 00:14:42,826
the boys took part.

226
00:14:43,396 --> 00:14:47,806
A blanket ban trades a working tool for
vague rules and years of litigation.

227
00:14:49,386 --> 00:14:51,136
Now the strongest case against.

228
00:14:51,946 --> 00:14:55,266
The lie that does the damage is the one
that tells an innocent person

229
00:14:55,286 --> 00:14:57,456
the evidence has already
convicted him.

230
00:14:57,906 --> 00:15:01,926
In the lab, a false witness nearly
doubled false confessions, and

231
00:15:01,946 --> 00:15:05,366
a review of the research puts the odds
of a false confession at roughly

232
00:15:05,386 --> 00:15:06,816
three to four times higher.

233
00:15:07,246 --> 00:15:09,336
People watching the tape can't
tell the difference.

234
00:15:09,946 --> 00:15:11,656
Children are the most exposed.

235
00:15:12,066 --> 00:15:15,746
In this case, five teenagers'
statements, contradicting each other

236
00:15:15,766 --> 00:15:19,786
and the scene, convicted them twice
without physical evidence, while

237
00:15:19,806 --> 00:15:23,556
the man whose DNA was on her sock
stayed free that summer.

238
00:15:24,226 --> 00:15:27,496
And English police have worked
without the lie for decades.

239
00:15:29,096 --> 00:15:30,146
Here's our verdict.

240
00:15:30,316 --> 00:15:33,936
On the Exonerated Five: the statements
should never have convicted

241
00:15:33,956 --> 00:15:38,276
them. But the lie told to Yusef Salaam
was only one part of what went

242
00:15:38,296 --> 00:15:42,776
wrong. Their ages, hours of
questioning nobody recorded, and a tape

243
00:15:42,796 --> 00:15:44,756
that caught only the end did more.

244
00:15:44,776 --> 00:15:47,836
Our confidence is eighty-five percent.

245
00:15:47,856 --> 00:15:51,856
What would change our mind: reliable
evidence, beyond the statements,

246
00:15:51,876 --> 00:15:56,186
that any of the five took part in
the attack on Trisha Meili.

247
00:16:00,546 --> 00:16:06,026
On children: no. Police should not be
allowed to lie to a child about

248
00:16:06,046 --> 00:16:09,816
the evidence, or promise
leniency they can't give.

249
00:16:10,526 --> 00:16:12,406
Our confidence is eighty-five percent.

250
00:16:12,426 --> 00:16:17,346
What would change our mind: evidence
from the states that have banned

251
00:16:17,366 --> 00:16:22,706
it that the laws cost true confessions in
serious cases, without reducing

252
00:16:22,726 --> 00:16:23,796
false ones.

253
00:16:26,896 --> 00:16:32,296
And for adults. Our verdict: police
shouldn't be allowed to lie about

254
00:16:32,316 --> 00:16:36,456
the evidence. A confession that
follows that lie should be presumed

255
00:16:36,476 --> 00:16:42,426
unreliable, for adults too, as it now
is for children in ten states.

256
00:16:43,076 --> 00:16:47,296
Our confidence is lower, sixty-five
percent, because adults are less

257
00:16:47,316 --> 00:16:51,796
vulnerable, and the evidence of harm
comes mostly from the lab and

258
00:16:51,816 --> 00:16:56,256
from the young. What would change our
mind: evidence from England

259
00:16:56,276 --> 00:17:01,296
or elsewhere that serious crimes go
unsolved because police can't

260
00:17:01,316 --> 00:17:01,626
bluff.

261
00:17:04,726 --> 00:17:06,536
That's our verdict.

262
00:17:07,166 --> 00:17:10,496
It's open, which means it can change.

263
00:17:11,266 --> 00:17:14,736
If you think we got it wrong, make
your case in the comments.

264
00:17:15,366 --> 00:17:18,356
We'll answer the strongest one
in the next episode.

265
00:17:21,906 --> 00:17:27,026
Next case: in 1990, a young woman in
Florida collapsed at home and

266
00:17:27,046 --> 00:17:30,626
never recovered. Her husband said she
wouldn't have wanted to be kept

267
00:17:30,646 --> 00:17:35,516
alive like that. Her parents disputed
it, and said she was still aware.

268
00:17:35,946 --> 00:17:39,666
Their fight over her feeding tube went
on for seven years, through

269
00:17:39,686 --> 00:17:41,916
the courts, Congress and
the White House.

270
00:17:42,406 --> 00:17:45,016
Who should decide when to stop
keeping someone alive?
